WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.340
Price your packages, pitch well, and agree every job in writing.

2
00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:08.720
This is the video for module two of Freelancing Fundamentals.

3
00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:19.510
Clients rarely want to buy hours. They want a finished result. But behind every price you need one number only you see: your minimum hourly rate.

4
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.530
You will turn your service into packages the client can choose from.

5
00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:28.260
You will learn a short pitch that gets replies.

6
00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:32.710
And you will agree every job in writing before you start.

7
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:41.320
Your minimum rate is the lowest amount per working hour that keeps your business alive. Here is how Amaka works it out.

8
00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:48.500
First, her income target. She wants to take home about 250,000 naira a month.

9
00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:56.600
Second, she adds business costs: data, power and fuel, and saving for a laptop. That is 40,000.

10
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.710
Together that is 290,000 naira.

11
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:11.580
Third, she divides by her 72 billable hours. That is about 4,028 naira, which she rounds up to 4,100 an hour.

12
00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:25.030
Twenty descriptions take her about 8 hours with checks and a revision. 8 times 4,100 is 32,800, so she prices the package at 35,000 naira.

13
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:35.900
Three sizes help the client choose how much to buy, instead of whether to buy at all. Basic is 10 descriptions for about 18,000 naira.

14
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:45.660
Standard is 20 descriptions for about 35,000. Many clients pick the middle package, so make it the one you most want to sell.

15
00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:54.820
Premium adds more work and a second revision round, for about 55,000. Check every price against your minimum rate.

16
00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:06.010
Each package should also say what is outside it, for example product photography not included. This protects you when a small extra is really a new job.

17
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:11.680
The goal of one message is not to close a sale. It is to get a reply.

18
00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:20.050
For example, Ifeoma in Enugu wrote to a home bakery. She noticed their cake posts had no prices or ordering steps.

19
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:27.960
She offered one service: short Instagram captions that include the price, size and a simple order step.

20
00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:32.860
She showed proof with one sample link, not her whole life story.

21
00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:43.560
And she asked for a tiny yes: may I rewrite three of your captions, at no cost? Keep free samples small, about 30 to 45 minutes at most.

22
00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:54.660
Busy people miss messages. Follow up once after 3 or 4 days, send a final note about a week later, then move on. Respect any no immediately.

23
00:02:55.910 --> 00:03:04.830
After a short discovery call, where you ask more and talk less, send a written scope the same day. Scope means exactly what work is included.

24
00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:15.640
List the deliverables, what the client provides, the deadline and revisions, what is not included, the price and payment terms, and who owns the final work.

25
00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:21.430
End with: if this is correct, please reply Agreed and send the deposit, and I will start.

26
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.710
A clear Agreed in writing is your record of the deal.

27
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:36.240
A deposit is part payment before you start. Many freelancers ask new clients for 50 percent up front, and the balance before final files.

28
00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:39.430
Only then does paid work begin.

29
00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:58.680
Watch for scope creep, when a job slowly grows through small extra requests. Zainab in Abuja agreed 12 posts for 60,000 naira. When the client asked for a flyer and 3 more posts, she sent a change request for 27,000 naira, and the client agreed.

30
00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:07.950
To remember. Your rate is your target plus costs, divided by billable hours. Price packages from your hours, rounded up.

31
00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:15.300
Pitch in four short parts. Get a written scope and a deposit before work. And price every extra request.

32
00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:22.630
Now build your price sheet with three packages, send your first ten pitches, and create your scope template.

33
00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.920
Continue with the lessons below.
